    "faq": {
      "type": "section-faq",
      "settings": {
        "title": "Frequently Asked Questions",
        "align_text": "text-left",
        "text_size": 1,
        "height": "margin--medium",
        "width": "wrapper--narrow",
        "text_color": "text--neutral",
        "bg": "palette--light bg--neutral"
      },
      "blocks": {
        "faq_q1": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "title": "How do I use K18 for the first time, step by step?",
            "content": "<p>Shampoo your hair (no conditioner) → towel-dry to damp → emulsify 1–2 pumps of K18 in your palms → apply from ends to roots → wait exactly 4 minutes → style as usual. Do not rinse out. That's the complete routine. Repeat every wash for your first 4–6 washes for maximum repair.</p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_q2": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Can I use K18 every time I wash my hair?",
            "content": "<p>Yes — and for your first 4–6 washes, you should. K18 repair is progressive and cumulative: every application reconnects more broken keratin bonds, so using it consistently during the initial phase delivers dramatically better results than occasional use. After the initial repair phase, switch to maintenance mode: every 3–4 washes is sufficient.</p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_q3": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Does K18 work on rebonded or chemically straightened hair?",
            "content": "<p>Yes. K18 is safe to use on rebonded, relaxed, and chemically straightened hair. It repairs internal structural damage without affecting the straightening results. In fact, rebonded hair is particularly well-suited to K18 because chemical straightening causes significant bond disruption — exactly what K18 is designed to address.</p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_q4": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Can I use K18 with my regular conditioner?",
            "content": "<p>Not on the same wash in the same step. On K18 wash days, skip rinse-out conditioner before applying K18 — conditioner creates a film on the hair that blocks K18 penetration. You can use your regular conditioner on non-K18 wash days, or you can use the K18 Damage Shield pH Protective Conditioner after shampooing and then follow with K18 as directed.</p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_q5": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Is K18 suitable for all hair types?",
            "content": "<p>Yes. K18 is formulated to work on all hair types and textures — straight, wavy, curly, coily, fine, thick, Asian, and everything in between. It's particularly effective for hair that's been chemically processed or heat-damaged, regardless of natural hair type.</p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_q6": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "title": "How is K18 different from a regular hair mask or deep conditioner?",
            "content": "<p>A regular hair mask or deep conditioner works on the outside surface of the hair shaft, temporarily smoothing the cuticle and adding moisture. K18 works inside the hair cortex, physically reconnecting broken keratin chains at the molecular level. It's structural repair versus surface conditioning — both are useful, but K18 addresses damage that traditional masks cannot touch.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
